## Source Quality Tiers

**ALWAYS prefer higher tiers. Reject low-quality sources.**

| Tier | Sources | Trust |
|------|---------|-------|
| Tier 1 | Official docs, RFCs, language specs, framework changelogs, GitHub organization repos | Always prefer |
| Tier 2 | GitHub repos ≥500 stars, maintained within 12 months, authored by known contributors | High trust |
| Tier 3 | Conference talks (JSConf, RustConf, etc.), established tech blogs (Vercel, Cloudflare, etc.), curated architecture SO answers | Use carefully |
| Tier 4 | Tutorial sites (Tutorialspoint, W3Schools, etc.), "for beginners" tagged content, paid courses | Ignore |

**Minimum per topic:** ≥2 Tier-1 sources AND ≥2 Tier-2 sources.

---

## Research Process (per topic)

For each topic in your cycle:

1. **WebSearch** — run 2–3 targeted queries:
   - `"{{ .StackName }} {topic} best practices 2026"`
   - `"{{ .FrameworkVersion }} {topic} architecture patterns"`
   - `"site:github.com {{ .StackName }} {topic} stars:>500"`

2. **WebFetch** — fetch the top 2–3 official documentation pages relevant to this topic.

3. **Bash (git clone)** — for one exemplary Tier-2 GitHub repo per topic:
   ```bash
   git clone --depth=1 {repo-url} /tmp/oracle-research/{topic}-{repo-name}
   # Then read key architectural files: ls, cat README.md, find . -name "*.ts" | head -20, etc.
   ```
   Clean up after reading: `rm -rf /tmp/oracle-research/{topic}-{repo-name}`

4. **Extract** — synthesize what you found into the output format below.

